If a presentation of the power domain enumerates the canonical power-domain neighbourhoods, then the flattening combinator relates the double-power neighbourhood coded by n to the power neighbourhood coded by m exactly when every index of every list coded inside n is contained, as a base neighbourhood, in some index of the list coded by m.

A coded outer neighbourhood is a member of the source, and a coded inner neighbourhood is a member of it, so the relation supplies a covering neighbourhood, which lies under a generator of the target. Conversely, a member of the source lies under a coded neighbourhood, a neighbourhood inside that member lies under a generator of the corresponding list, and the hypothesis supplies a covering code whose inclusion composes with that containment.
